<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Langensalza (1075)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1075">1075</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Homburg on the Unstrut</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Langensalza, also called the Battle of Homburg on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a>, was fought on 9 June 1075 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> rebellion against the Salian king Henry IV. Henry defeated the rebel Saxon nobility, whose leaders submitted to him later that year. The Saxon opposition revived soon afterwards during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-langensalza-1075.jpg" alt="Battle of Langensalza (1075)" width="320" height="568" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otakar Frankenberger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plan_of_the_attack_during_the_battle_of_Langensalza_(Homburg,_1075).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Henry IV inherited his quarrels with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> from his father, Emperor Henry III, whose long and costly stays at the Imperial Palace of Goslar had annoyed the local nobles. Attempts to restore royal rights over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> forests, to extend the crownlands and to raise the demands of the fisc met opposition, and a policy of building castles held by loyal, mainly Swabian officials bred resentment. The great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harzburg">Harzburg</a> became a symbol of imperial tyranny.</p><p>In 1070/71 Henry seized the title and property of Otto of Nordheim, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and kept Magnus Billung prisoner at Harzburg Castle. In 1073 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and princes organised a resistance, and in February 1074 Henry made concessions at Gerstungen. Saxon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> then sacked the Harzburg and scattered the bones of members of the royal family, which shocked the religious authorities and gave Henry a pretext for renewed war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The two sides met at Homburg on 9 June 1075. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> army of several thousand men was mostly on foot, made up of nobles and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> and some serfs, many of them poorly trained. Henry's army advanced in five ranks over the open ground south of the River Unstrut, and the Saxons rode out of their castle at Homburg, apparently leaving their foot soldiers behind.</p><p>The engagement was less a battle than a rout. A charge by the Swabians under Duke Rudolf almost at once broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> centre. The Saxon leaders fled on horseback, while the foot soldiers were slaughtered, and several thousand were reported drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Henry's army ravaged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> and Thuringian countryside, bringing starvation. The Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> asked for terms, and Henry demanded a short imprisonment for the leaders and the confiscation of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>. The rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, nobles and peasants walked barefoot between the ranks of the royal army to submit. At Goslar at Christmas Henry freed Otto of Nordheim and made him his viceroy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Langensalza (1075)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
